:: F10 {"position":"25,275","size":"100,100"}
Model answer: C. Incorporation into uric acid

You correctly identified <<print $corr10>> of 1 correct answers.<br>
You missed <<print $miss10>> correct answers.<br>
You selected <<print $bad10>> incorrect answers.

Missed correct answers: <<if $missList10 neq "">><<print $missList10>><<else>>None<</if>><br>
Incorrect selections: <<if $badList10 neq "">><<print $badList10>><<else>>None<</if>>

Birds primarily dispose of excess nitrogen by incorporating it into uric acid, which conserves water during excretion. Conversion to ketone bodies and glutamate are not disposal routes, and excretion as ammonia is characteristic of many aquatic organisms rather than chickens. Nitrogen metabolism therefore differs between birds and mammals, with uric acid synthesis serving as the major endpoint.

