Incorrect selections: <<if $badList4 neq "">><<print $badList4>><<else>>None<</if>>

An essential amino acid cannot be synthesised in sufficient quantities by the organism and must come from the diet. Essentiality is not restricted to growth, liver metabolism, or production by gut microbes. Nutritional requirements reflect the absence of complete biosynthetic pathways. Amino acid metabolism therefore depends on dietary supply for certain compounds while nonessential amino acids can be produced internally.

[[Continue->Q5]]


:: F5 {"position":"25,150","size":"100,100"}
Model answer: C. Corynebacterium glutamicum

You correctly identified <<print $corr5>> of 1 correct answers.<br>
You missed <<print $miss5>> correct answers.<br>
You selected <<print $bad5>> incorrect answers.

Missed correct answers: <<if $missList5 neq "">><<print $missList5>><<else>>None<</if>><br>
Incorrect selections: <<if $badList5 neq "">><<print $badList5>><<else>>None<</if>>

Corynebacterium glutamicum is a major industrial organism used for commercial glutamate production because its metabolism can be directed toward amino acid overproduction. Escherichia coli, Saccharomyces cerevisiae, and Bacillus subtilis have many industrial uses but are not the classic glutamate production platform. The process exploits carbon flux, nitrogen assimilation, and regulatory controls of amino acid biosynthesis pathways.

[[Continue->Q6]]


:: F6 {"position":"275,150","size":"100,100"}
Model answer: A. Leucine

You correctly identified <<print $corr6>> of 1 correct answers.<br>
You missed <<print $miss6>> correct answers.<br>
You selected <<print $bad6>> incorrect answers.

Missed correct answers: <<if $missList6 neq "">><<print $missList6>><<else>>None<</if>><br>
Incorrect selections: <<if $badList6 neq "">><<print $badList6>><<else>>None<</if>>

Leucine is a branched chain amino acid that strongly activates mTOR signalling and promotes muscle protein synthesis. Glycine, aspartate, and serine have important metabolic roles but are not the principal amino acid signal for this pathway. mTOR integrates nutrient availability with anabolic metabolism, coordinating translation, growth, and protein accumulation in response to amino acid status.

[[Continue->Q7]]


:: F7 {"position":"525,150","size":"100,100"}
Model answer: D. The urea cycle

You correctly identified <<print $corr7>> of 1 correct answers.<br>
You missed <<print $miss7>> correct answers.<br>
You selected <<print $bad7>> incorrect answers.

Missed correct answers: <<if $missList7 neq "">><<print $missList7>><<else>>None<</if>><br>
Incorrect selections: <<if $badList7 neq "">><<print $badList7>><<else>>None<</if>>

The urea cycle is the primary route for disposal of excess nitrogen in mammals. Amino acid catabolism generates ammonia that is converted into urea for safe excretion. The pentose phosphate pathway, TCA cycle, and fatty acid synthesis serve different metabolic functions and do not perform nitrogen disposal. This illustrates the integration of amino acid degradation with specialised nitrogen handling mechanisms.

[[Continue->Q8]]


:: F8 {"position":"775,150","size":"100,100"}
Model answer: B. Glutamate

You correctly identified <<print $corr8>> of 1 correct answers.<br>
You missed <<print $miss8>> correct answers.<br>
You selected <<print $bad8>> incorrect answers.

Missed correct answers: <<if $missList8 neq "">><<print $missList8>><<else>>None<</if>><br>
Incorrect selections: <<if $badList8 neq "">><<print $badList8>><<else>>None<</if>>

Glutamate is the key ligand for umami receptors and produces a stronger umami signal than inosinate or guanylate alone, although nucleotides can enhance the response synergistically. Citrate, lactate, and urea are not principal umami receptor ligands. Glutamate occupies a central position in amino acid metabolism, nitrogen transfer reactions, and flavour perception derived from protein-rich foods.

[[Continue->Q9]]


:: F9 {"position":"1025,150","size":"100,100"}
Model answer: C. Transamination

You correctly identified <<print $corr9>> of 1 correct answers.<br>
You missed <<print $miss9>> correct answers.<br>
You selected <<print $bad9>> incorrect answers.

Missed correct answers: <<if $missList9 neq "">><<print $missList9>><<else>>None<</if>><br>
Incorrect selections: <<if $badList9 neq "">><<print $badList9>><<else>>None<</if>>

Transamination connects amino acid metabolism with both nitrogen and carbon metabolism by transferring amino groups between amino acids and keto acids. This process links compounds such as pyruvate, oxaloacetate, and α-ketoglutarate to amino acid pools. β-oxidation, translation, and glycolysis have important functions but do not provide this direct integration of nitrogen handling with carbon skeleton interconversion.

